+# PayPal checkout contract
+
+What the browser, this server and PayPal each promise. Amounts cross the
+PayPal boundary only as decimal strings in dollars; everywhere inside the
+shop they are integer cents.
+
+## Amount conversion
+
+`centsToPayPalAmount(cents)` in `lib/paypal.js` is the one conversion:
+`4900 -> "49.00"`, `5 -> "0.05"`. `payPalAmountToCents` is its exact inverse
+(string-parsed, never floats) and is used to verify captured amounts.
+
+## Create order — `POST /api/paypal/orders`
+
+- Request: `{ items: [{ sku, qty }] }` only. Any `price`, `amount`, `total`
+  or `currency` the browser sends is ignored; the bag is priced from the
+  server catalog (known SKUs, integer quantities 1–10).
+- The shop order is created first. Its id goes to PayPal as
+  `purchase_units[0].custom_id` (and `invoice_id`), and the returned PayPal
+  order id is stored on the shop order as `payment_reference` under a unique
+  index — one PayPal order binds to exactly one shop order.
+- The PayPal amount is the catalog total via `centsToPayPalAmount`, in
+  `USD`, with `breakdown.item_total` and per-item `unit_amount`s that sum
+  to the total.
+- Response: `{ id: <PayPal order id>, orderId: <shop order id> }`. On a
+  PayPal error the shop order is marked failed and PayPal's status/issue is
+  returned instead; no PayPal order id is invented.
+
+## Capture — `POST /api/paypal/orders/:paypalOrderId/capture`
+
+- Paid means captured: the order is marked paid only when PayPal's capture
+  response (or, on `ORDER_ALREADY_CAPTURED`, the order read back from
+  PayPal) contains a capture with status `COMPLETED` under the purchase
+  unit whose `custom_id` is the shop order, for exactly the shop total and
+  currency. Buyer approval alone — including a capture response that just
+  shows the order still `CREATED` — is never payment.
+- Failures stay unpaid and unshipped, with PayPal's own status, `issue` and
+  `debug_id` in the response: a refused capture of an unapproved order
+  keeps its 4xx (e.g. 422 `ORDER_NOT_APPROVED`), `INSTRUMENT_DECLINED`
+  answers 402 with `restart: true` so the buyer can choose another way to
+  pay, and a `PENDING` capture answers 202 to wait for the webhook.
+  Amount/currency/`custom_id` mismatches answer 422 with the reason.
+- Settling claims `pending -> paid` atomically and fulfilment claims
+  `paid -> fulfilled` atomically, so capture, webhook and retries between
+  them ship exactly once. Capturing an already-settled order answers 200
+  with its current status and ships nothing more.
+
+## Webhooks — `POST /api/paypal/webhooks`
+
+- Every event is verified first via
+  `POST /v1/notifications/verify-webhook-signature` with the
+  `PAYPAL-*` transmission headers, `PAYPAL_WEBHOOK_ID` and the parsed body.
+  Anything but `verification_status: "SUCCESS"` answers 400 and changes
+  nothing.
+- Verified event ids are recorded once (unique key); a repeat delivery
+  answers 200 and changes nothing.
+- Only `PAYMENT.CAPTURE.COMPLETED` with status `COMPLETED` and the exact
+  shop total/currency can settle an order (through the same atomic claim).
+  `CHECKOUT.ORDER.APPROVED`, `PAYMENT.CAPTURE.PENDING` and all other events
+  are acknowledged (200) but never pay, ship, or move an order backwards.
+
+## Browser (Web SDK v6)
+
+`GET /api/paypal/config` exposes only the client id, the SDK script URL
+(sandbox unless `PAYPAL_ENV=live`) and, in sandbox, the test buyer country —
+never the secret. `onApprove` posts to the capture route and only shows the
+confirmation on a 2xx; an error (e.g. declined instrument) shows PayPal's
+reason and leaves the order unpaid.
